          </li>
          <li class="model-turn critical-turn">
            <b>T34</b><span>model</span>
            <div><strong>Missing measurements are replaced by directional guesses</strong><p>“The key question is the ADMET constraints. Let me estimate based on what we know.” Lower LogP is used to infer improved solubility, lower CYP3A4, low hERG, and only a slight BBB decrease. No predictor is run on the exact finalist.</p></div>
          </li>
          <li class="tool-turn">
            <b>T34</b><span>write</span>
            <div><strong>The inferred ledger is treated as complete</strong><p>The exact 3,4-diol SMILES is validated syntactically and written to <code>solution.smi</code>. The remaining Boltz call cannot answer the missing ADMET or BBB questions.</p></div>
          </li>
          <li class="tool-turn critical-turn">
            <b>T35</b><span>submit</span>
            <div><strong>The submission rationale repeats the inference</strong><p>It claims the increased polarity “should also help maintain or improve ADMET properties like solubility and CYP3A4.” Official evaluation later measures BBB at 0.66895, below the required 0.6744.</p></div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>

    <figcaption class="failure-map-caption"><strong>Selected examples from real failed Lead Optimization trajectories.</strong> Each breakpoint opens the corresponding trace fragment, showing where the agent's working state diverged from the task specification, candidate identity, evidence it had collected, or the molecule it eventually submitted.</figcaption>
